The multiplicities are $3$ and $1$, so the degree is $4$.
The leading term is $-2x^{4}$.
An even degree with a negative leading coefficient gives $y\to-\infty$ as $x\to\infty$ and as $x\to-\infty$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-3.1, xmax=3.8, ymin=-60.5, ymax=70.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-2.73:3.35] {-2*(x+1)*(x+1)*(x+1)*(x-3)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$x=0$ has multiplicity $2$ and $x=-4$ has multiplicity $2$.
Both multiplicities are even, so the curve touches the axis at each and turns back.
$y=\left(x(x+4)\right)^{2}\ge0$ for all $x$, so the curve never crosses the axis.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5.4, xmax=1.4, ymin=-3.5, ymax=22.5, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-4.95:0.95] {x*x*(x+4)*(x+4)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
